QSOC: bring out LSR.data_ready of obi_uart as a level output, for a DMA RX request.

Reason: the QSOC DMA (dma_axi32) takes a level request per peripheral. obi_uart has one OR'd
interrupt line and no DMA request pin. LSR[0] (data ready) is a registered level that is 1 while the
RX holding register or the RX FIFO holds an unread character and falls 2 clocks after the last read
(1 clock with the FIFOs off). It is the same rule as RXRDY of a 16550 in DMA mode 0. Using it as
the RX request removes the dependence on IER, on the RX timeout and on the one-clock RX trigger pulse.

What it changes: one added output port, dma_rx_req_o, on obi_uart_register and on obi_uart.
It is read only (assign dma_rx_req_o = reg_q.LSR.data_ready). No existing port, register or
behaviour changes. Checked by lock-step co-simulation of the original and the patched module: 30 random
seeds, 90,000,990 clocks, every APB response, irq, txd and the whole register file identical.

Not covered: the TX direction (LSR.thr_empty stays 1 after the first THR write), so TX keeps using INT.
This is not the earlier patch 0001-add-dma-request-lines, which used rx_fifo_trigger.

Applied by util/vendor_ip.py (git apply -p1 --directory vendor/pulp-platform/obi_peripherals).
Upstream base: pulp-platform/obi_peripherals v0.1.1, 079894a6cbd4e544c477643d184a4c340bd4e391.
Used by: design/uart

diff --git a/hw/obi_uart/obi_uart.sv b/hw/obi_uart/obi_uart.sv
